4
00:00:19,279 --> 00:00:21,590
Welcome to Software Engineering Radio.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,390
I'm your host Gregory Caphammer.

6
00:00:24,390 --> 00:00:27,029
Today's guest is Casper Wakovski.

7
00:00:27,029 --> 00:00:29,589
He's a senior developer advocate at Quadrant.

8
00:00:29,589 --> 00:00:34,150
Quadrant is an open- source vector database and vector search similarity search engine.

9
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:35,910
Casper, welcome to the show.

10
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:37,911
Hello, Greg. Thanks for the invitation.

11
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:41,590
Hey, I'm really glad today that we get a chance to talk about Quadrant.

12
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:49,350
It's a vector database and we're going to learn more about how it helps us to solve a number of key problems. So, are you ready to dive in?

13
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:51,670
Definitely. Okay.

14
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:59,270
So, we're going to start with an introduction to vector databases and we're going to cover a couple highle concepts and then later dive into some additional details.

15
00:00:59,270 --> 00:01:03,111
So let's start with the simple question of what is a vector database? Can you tell us more?

16
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:05,270
Yes, of course.

17
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:09,270
First of all, I think vector search engine is a more appropriate term.

18
00:01:09,270 --> 00:01:13,429
A search is the main functionality that this kind of tools provide.

19
00:01:13,429 --> 00:01:26,550
Nevertheless, it's a service that can efficiently store and handle highdimensional vectors for the purposes of similarity search and similarity of the vectors is defined by the closeness of the vectors in that space.

20
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:29,670
So vector databases are built to make that process efficient.

21
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:32,230
Okay.

22
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:37,429
So a vector database helps us to achieve vector search or vector similarity search. Is that the right way to think about it?

23
00:01:37,439 --> 00:01:39,670
Exactly. Okay.

24
00:01:39,670 --> 00:01:43,350
Now one of the things you mentioned was the word vector and then you said highdimensional.

25
00:01:43,350 --> 00:01:46,630
Can you briefly explain what highdimensional data is?

26
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:48,710
Yes.

27
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:54,710
In case of vector embeddings, we describe them as high dimensional because they usually have at least a few hundreds of dimensions.

28
00:01:54,710 --> 00:01:58,469
Typically, no more than eight or 9,000 dimensions.

29
00:01:58,469 --> 00:02:09,430
And it's definitely not like high dimensional data if you are seasoned data expert, but it's relatively high because it's hard to imagine, hard to interpret for a regular human.

30
00:02:09,430 --> 00:02:11,910
So, this is the range that we are usually operating in.

31
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:13,910
Okay, that's helpful.

32
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:16,070
Now, you mentioned the term embedding a moment ago.

33
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:19,589
Can you talk briefly about the concept of a vector embedding?

34
00:02:19,599 --> 00:02:21,670
Sure.

35
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:31,990
So, vector embeddings are just numerical representations of the input data and the main idea is that they keep the semantic meaning of the input data that was used to generate them.

36
00:02:31,990 --> 00:02:42,229
And if we have two different vectors which are similar in some way, then we assume that the objects that are used to generate them are also similar in their nature.

37
00:02:42,239 --> 00:02:48,390
And vector embeddings actually enabled semantic search that can understand not only the presence of particular keywords

38
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:59,270
but also user intents and more importantly they enabled search on unstructured data that was impossible to be processed in the past.

39
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:03,190
So let me see if I'm understanding the workflow correctly.

40
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:13,190
is the idea that I take something like source code or images or documents and then I convert those to embeddings and then I store those in the vector database.

41
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:15,110
Am I thinking about this the right way?

42
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:24,470
Yes, that's the correct way and the main idea is that this vector embeddings are generated by neural networks which were trained solely for that purposes.

43
00:03:24,470 --> 00:03:35,910
So that's also why we quite often describe vector search as neural search because it requires some sort of neural networks to encode the data into this numerical representations.

44
00:03:35,910 --> 00:03:42,309
Some of our listeners may not have previously used a vector database or done some type of vector similarity search.

45
00:03:42,309 --> 00:03:48,630
Can you tell us a little bit more about how you know when your project actually needs a vector database?

46
00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:50,550
There are no strict criteria here of course.

47
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:57,830
But generally if you build any kind of search mechanism and whenever you want to add this semantic search capabilities into it

48
00:03:57,830 --> 00:04:06,229
then you should have a look at vector databases because they just make the deployment and the maintenance of this kind of projects easier.

49
00:04:06,229 --> 00:04:15,030
Also, when you want to implement search over some data modality that can be processed with traditional search means such as images or audio,

50
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:24,070
then you definitely need to use semantic search because that's probably the only approach to search on unstructured data like this.

51
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:33,990
And of course, if you have just a few examples of documents that never change, then vector databases might be just an additional overhead in your project.

52
00:04:33,990 --> 00:04:43,430
So then maybe implementing a semantic search directly into your application and embedding this documents directly into the source code makes sense.

53
00:04:43,430 --> 00:04:51,749
But in general if you have data that changes frequently you should be using vector database to implement semantic search.

54
00:04:51,749 --> 00:04:59,270
Especially nowadays vector databases come along well with large language models because in both cases

55
00:04:59,270 --> 00:05:07,029
we expect natural language like interactions and we are not necessarily looking only at the presence of the keywords.

56
00:05:07,029 --> 00:05:15,830
So if you build a system that exposes a conversational like interface then vector databases might be really important to achieve that quickly.

57
00:05:15,830 --> 00:05:23,749
So you mentioned the idea of keyword search engine and we've already talked about the concept of a similarity search engine.

58
00:05:23,749 --> 00:05:28,469
How are these two types of search engines similar to and different from each other?

59
00:05:28,469 --> 00:05:31,510
So historically search was tied solely to textual data.

60
00:05:31,510 --> 00:05:39,110
We didn't have any other means that would allow us to search over images or any different data modality.

61
00:05:39,110 --> 00:05:46,629
And since we are only focusing on text, we developed some specific methods that were dividing the text into meaningful pieces,

62
00:05:46,629 --> 00:05:56,629
not necessarily specific words, but we were also converting them into their root forms through stemming or some different leatization techniques.

63
00:05:56,629 --> 00:06:05,990
And then we were just building inverted indexes that were supporting the lexical search which was based on the present on some specific keywords.

64
00:06:05,990 --> 00:06:12,790
And imagine you had a very specific use case in which two different words could describe the same object, the same phenomena.

65
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:15,909
Then you would need to manually maintain a list of synonyms.

66
00:06:15,919 --> 00:06:20,870
So this process will convert all the different synonyms into the same form.

67
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:29,670
So that means a lot of effort, maybe even building a whole team of people focusing on search and improving search relevance.

68
00:06:29,670 --> 00:06:34,230
And semantic search is slightly different because it based on neural networks.

69
00:06:34,230 --> 00:06:40,870
And this neural networks are trained to understand the meaning of the words and whole sentences.

70
00:06:40,870 --> 00:06:47,749
And that means you don't necessarily need to use the same terminology as the people who created the documents you are searching over.

71
00:06:47,759 --> 00:06:59,110
But you can also express yourself however you want assuming the model was trained properly for that particular language and still get significantly better results even

72
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:06,070
though you can't really speak the same language as the domain experts who created the whole database.

73
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:15,110
So that's the main difference and also historically we were using tools such as elastic search or open search or anything based on lucine actually to support lexical search.

74
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:20,390
Right now, vector databases are just another different search paradigm.

75
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:23,350
Thanks. That response was really helpful.

76
00:07:23,350 --> 00:07:31,110
So, I want to turn our attention now to Quadrant and then briefly discuss some of the types of applications people can build with Quadrant.

77
00:07:31,110 --> 00:07:34,950
So, at the start of the show, you said that Quadrant was a vector similarity search engine.

78
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:40,790
Can you tell us a few of the key features that Quadrant provides and what developers can actually build with Quadrant?

79
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,430
Of course.

80
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:50,469
So quadrant provides a very efficient and lightweight search engine that can handle different types of vectors dense sparse

81
00:07:50,469 --> 00:07:59,029
and multi vector representations and we also support all the existing optimization techniques which are relevant for that space.

82
00:07:59,029 --> 00:08:13,270
So just to name a few we support different kinds of quantization such as product scalar and binary quantization that helps to reduce the amount of memory required to run semantic search at scale.

83
00:08:13,270 --> 00:08:19,110
We can also store the data on disk if you prefer to reduce the cost of running semantic search and you are okay

84
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:31,189
with higher latency or GPU based indexing if you really care about the time spent on building some helpful data structures that we use to make that search efficient.

85
00:08:31,189 --> 00:08:38,070
And one important feature or functionality of quiron is that it allows to keep multiple vectors per each point along with some metadata

86
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:48,949
that can be also used for filtering which is kind of important because a typical use case requires you not only to search based on the semantics of the data.

87
00:08:48,949 --> 00:08:50,630
Imagine you are looking for the best restaurants nearby you.

88
00:08:50,630 --> 00:08:54,470
You definitely don't want to see restaurants from the other part of the globe.

89
00:08:54,470 --> 00:08:58,630
You definitely want to restrict your search to a specific area.

90
00:08:58,630 --> 00:09:09,750
So you don't need to travel to have your dinner and that's exactly where metadata filtering is important and it's implemented in a slightly unique way compared to the other vector databases.

91
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:21,430
So I would say those are the main features of quadrant and when it comes to different applications what quadrant implements is actually an approximation of nearest neighbor search.

92
00:09:21,430 --> 00:09:28,230
KN&amp;N K nearest newest neighbors is a quite well-known algorithm for those who have any kind of experience in machine learning.

93
00:09:28,230 --> 00:09:36,070
That's probably the most basic ML algorithm that exists and it's known for its versatility.

94
00:09:36,070 --> 00:09:47,509
However, it's really hard to scale it up just because there at inference time KN&amp;N requires us to compare the distance to all the vectors we have within the system.

95
00:09:47,509 --> 00:09:54,550
So quadrant as well all the other vector databases just approximate nearest neighbor search.

96
00:09:54,550 --> 00:09:57,590
So it's can be implemented in sublinear time.

97
00:09:57,590 --> 00:10:05,990
But that also means that we can solve variety of problems that pure KN&amp;N could also solve obviously semantic search.

98
00:10:05,990 --> 00:10:17,269
So if you have an existing application and just want to enhance it with additional semantic search capabilities that's something you could definitely implement with Quadrant.

99
00:10:17,269 --> 00:10:19,670
However, vector search enables way more than just pure search.

100
00:10:19,670 --> 00:10:29,990
Cuz since we have the similarity measure, we can also perform a very simple classification pipeline using the same approach.

101
00:10:29,990 --> 00:10:38,310
Because if we just select the top 10 closest documents or top 10 closest documents in general, by running a simple voting procedure,

102
00:10:38,310 --> 00:10:50,630
we can just select the most common class among all these closest neighbors and assign the class to a new observation just because the majority of observations in its neighborhood belong to it.

103
00:10:50,630 --> 00:10:58,389
And the similarity measure is also interesting on its own just because you can use it to detect anomalies.

104
00:10:58,389 --> 00:11:02,870
Let's say you know the distribution of the queries you typically get into your system.

105
00:11:02,870 --> 00:11:11,829
Then you can also detect that a particular query is just way below the expected range of similarity.

106
00:11:11,829 --> 00:11:21,110
and then maybe add a human in the loop component just to react to that particular observation because that may indicate that somebody is just trying to hack your system for example.

107
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:25,910
And last but not least, recommendation engines.

108
00:11:25,910 --> 00:11:31,269
If you have positive and negative examples such as movies that somebody liked or disliked,

109
00:11:31,269 --> 00:11:42,310
you can also use multiple vectors and serve recommendations based on this multiple objects that that particular person has interacted with in the past.

110
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:44,150
Thanks for that explanation.

111
00:11:44,150 --> 00:11:50,389
It was really helpful and I appreciated in particular you commenting on the idea of a recommendation engine.

112
00:11:50,389 --> 00:11:56,710
I wanted to think about recommendation maybe from a perspective that would be accessible for our listeners.

113
00:11:56,710 --> 00:12:01,030
So for example, if we're thinking about software testing and I have a test case that fails,

114
00:12:01,030 --> 00:12:13,030
how could I use semantic search to find the other test cases that are like the failing test case that might perhaps also fail so that I don't have to run the whole regression test suite?

115
00:12:13,030 --> 00:12:15,030
Can you walk us through that type of example?

116
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:18,790
Yeah, I can definitely try to describe the approach.

117
00:12:18,790 --> 00:12:20,870
However, I can't promise that it's going to work.

118
00:12:20,870 --> 00:12:28,389
But definitely there are some embedding models that can work with uh source code different languages at the same time as well.

119
00:12:28,389 --> 00:12:39,509
And I can imagine that somebody could just encode all the test cases from the suits just to capture the meaning of a particular test.

120
00:12:39,509 --> 00:12:45,590
And then if you have a let's say a quadrant instance and a collection with all these representations of all the test cases

121
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:56,949
you have then you can try to find for the nearest neighbors of failing test case that you just encountered and then try to run them first to evaluate whether they are also failing.

122
00:12:56,949 --> 00:13:09,509
So that might one of the approaches to that and since you would be using an embedding model that was trained solely to support code search then I would expect it to work properly just

123
00:13:09,509 --> 00:13:19,509
because the nature of the code is slightly different from natural language like processing cuz here it's not only about the convention of

124
00:13:19,509 --> 00:13:25,190
how we name our variables methods and classes but it's more about the structure and the syntax of the code itself.

125
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:33,030
So this kind of model should capture more nuances of the data and hopefully recognize this problematic test cases early on.

126
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:35,269
Okay, that makes sense.

127
00:13:35,269 --> 00:13:43,990
So in this case, I have to find the source code of the test cases and then I have to produce an embedding of the source code of the test cases,

128
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:50,710
store that inside a quadrant and then use it to help me to find the k nearest neighbors associated with that test case. Am I thinking about that the right way?

129
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:52,310
Exactly.

130
00:13:52,310 --> 00:13:54,310
That's the process I would suggest to test.

131
00:13:54,310 --> 00:13:58,069
Unfortunately, that's an interesting use case, but I haven't tried it on my own yet.

132
00:13:58,079 --> 00:13:59,910
Okay.

133
00:13:59,910 --> 00:14:03,350
I wanted to talk briefly about some of the other use cases that you mentioned a moment ago.

134
00:14:03,350 --> 00:14:10,629
So, you might want to do semantic search for like documentation, maybe markdown files or PDFs.

135
00:14:10,629 --> 00:14:16,150
What do you have to do to put the markdown file or the PDF in the right format before you embed it?

136
00:14:16,150 --> 00:14:21,509
Can you talk a little bit further about how to do semantic search for various types of documents?

137
00:14:21,519 --> 00:14:23,350
Of course.

138
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:30,150
So markdown files are actually the easiest case cuz here we have just text with some additional formatting apply on top of that.

139
00:14:30,150 --> 00:14:42,230
And the main challenge here is that we can't easily just put a whole document into an embedding model and expect it to encode the whole meaning of that document within a few hundreds of dimensions.

140
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:49,509
That would be like a perfect compression mechanism if you can just put a whole book inside of such a short vector.

141
00:14:49,509 --> 00:14:59,189
So definitely what we need to do is to chunk that into meaningful pieces and the way we chunk really depends on the data we have.

142
00:14:59,189 --> 00:15:01,430
As we are speaking about markdown files,

143
00:15:01,430 --> 00:15:10,150
you typically have some headers and paragraphs at least maybe some list tables etc. So if you want to chant your markdown files properly

144
00:15:10,150 --> 00:15:24,550
to bring all the context possible then you probably need to take all the headers way down to the particular paragraph you're encoding just to keep the traction of all the headers that have appeared so far.

145
00:15:24,550 --> 00:15:33,990
So then you are building more trust that the embedding will capture all the information that it has to capture in order to keep the meaning of that particular piece.

146
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,750
However, it's pretty tricky.

147
00:15:35,750 --> 00:15:40,310
Like there is no single method that you can use for chunking.

148
00:15:40,310 --> 00:15:48,949
The naive way of just using a fixed window length doesn't usually work because chunking itself like imagine you are reading a book

149
00:15:48,949 --> 00:15:58,790
and if you just start from a random paragraph, it's really hard to say what was the meaning of that paragraph in the context of a whole book or just a chapter.

150
00:15:58,790 --> 00:16:05,269
So chunking usually requires some additional means and some knowledge about the data that we have in order to be done properly.

151
00:16:05,269 --> 00:16:12,710
And once you chunked the documents, I assume you can tell what's the best way of how to do that.

152
00:16:12,710 --> 00:16:18,550
If you know the dogs to working with, then you need to pass all the chunks through the embedding model of your choice.

153
00:16:18,550 --> 00:16:20,710
And there are plenty of open- source embedding models available.

154
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:33,749
I really recommend having a look at sentence transformers which is a Python library that exposes multiple open-source models some of them even multilingual so you can work with multiple languages at the same time or

155
00:16:33,759 --> 00:16:45,590
if you prefer SAS then open AI or cohhere are providing this kind of models too and once you have the embeddings you send this embeddings along

156
00:16:45,590 --> 00:16:56,710
with the metadata which is usually the input data that was used to generate this particular vector to quadrant So that's the typical approach and once you have this ingestion pipeline in place you can start searching over it.

157
00:16:56,720 --> 00:16:58,629
Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

158
00:16:58,629 --> 00:17:09,429
I know a moment ago you mentioned the idea of using sentence transformers and in my experience sentence transformers is something that I get from hugging face and download to my computer.

159
00:17:09,429 --> 00:17:11,270
Am I remembering that correctly?

160
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:15,189
Yes, it's a typical approach at least when you are experimenting.

161
00:17:15,189 --> 00:17:22,390
Of course, you can use hug interface directly because they have this inference endpoints.

162
00:17:22,390 --> 00:17:30,310
So I can imagine like in some cases you can't really run this models on your own infrastructure or just your own laptop because it won't be that effective.

163
00:17:30,310 --> 00:17:43,669
And in that case you can easily use for example hugging phase inference endpoints to run them on their infrastructure or more recently we have introduced this kind of feature cloud inference into quadrant cloud.

164
00:17:43,669 --> 00:17:48,230
So you can also just send the raw data and encode it server side.

165
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:50,470
Aha.

166
00:17:50,470 --> 00:17:55,190
Now in a moment I want to compare and contrast Quadrant to other types of databases.

167
00:17:55,190 --> 00:18:04,150
But before I do that, can you briefly comment on how Quadrant and the type of system that you build with it is similar to and different from retrieval augmented generation?

168
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:08,390
Quadrant may be a part of retrieval augmented generation pipelines.

169
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:15,750
So retrieval augmented generation is all about bringing a relevant context into the prompt that we send to the LLMs.

170
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:23,510
Obviously LLMs have some disadvantages cuz they were trained on some specific data sets.

171
00:18:23,510 --> 00:18:27,190
And even though at the first glance it may look like they know everything.

172
00:18:27,190 --> 00:18:33,430
They would definitely not know anything about the internal processes of your organization or maybe some personal data of yours.

173
00:18:33,430 --> 00:18:35,669
Well, they definitely shouldn't know that.

174
00:18:35,669 --> 00:18:43,029
So the whole idea behind retrieval augmented generation is to use the retrieval component which might be semantic search

175
00:18:43,039 --> 00:18:51,029
for example to find some relevant information and to automatically add it to the prompt that you sent to the LLM.

176
00:18:51,039 --> 00:19:00,950
So let's say you start with a user's question that was sent directly to your system and instead of using that prompt that's query directly

177
00:19:00,950 --> 00:19:09,669
and sending that to the LLM you use it as if it was a query to your retrieval system and that's why semantic search makes a lot of sense

178
00:19:09,669 --> 00:19:19,430
because we have this natural conversations with LLMs and then quadrant in that scenario would just find some relevant documents parts of the documents

179
00:19:19,430 --> 00:19:29,270
that it finds to be important to answer that particular particular question then retrieve argumented generation would just build another prompt including your original question

180
00:19:29,270 --> 00:19:35,029
and this document retrieved from the database and ask the model to answer based solely on this document.

181
00:19:35,029 --> 00:19:47,990
So perfectly it should reduce hallucinations and also make sure that the model can rely on its language capabilities not on the internal state or knowledge that it has.

182
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:49,590
Okay.

183
00:19:49,590 --> 00:19:52,150
So, if I'm understanding you correctly,

184
00:19:52,150 --> 00:20:06,549
the idea is you can use Quadrant in order to find a document and similar documents that are important to you and then you can put that into the context window of the LLM which will then help the LLM do a better job at whatever task you've given it.

185
00:20:06,549 --> 00:20:08,070
Did I explain it in the right way?

186
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:10,150
Exactly.

187
00:20:10,150 --> 00:20:23,270
That's the process of virtual augmented generation and that also helps the LLM to rely on its summarization capabilities or information extraction capabilities not using it as if it was a search engine on its own.

188
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:25,352
Okay. Thank you. That was fantastic.

189
00:20:25,352 --> 00:20:36,470
Now in a moment I want to begin our conversation about how Quadrant was implemented and then we're going to spend some time talking about how you actually benchmark the performance of Quadrant.

190
00:20:36,470 --> 00:20:47,990
But before we do that, our listeners may be aware of the fact that we're talking about databases and they may be familiar with other types of databases like a relational database or a NoSQL database or a document database.

191
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:56,149
Could you overview the landscape of different types of databases and then tell us a little bit about how Quadrant fits into that landscape?

192
00:20:56,159 --> 00:20:57,909
Of course.

