00:37:21,190 --> 00:37:32,710
And in fact, since you just said the phrase vector databases in general, I think it might be appropriate for us to at least briefly compare Quadrant to some of the other vector databases that our listeners might be familiar with.

341
00:37:32,710 --> 00:37:41,510
So, for example, they might have heard of PG Vector or Pine Cone, or maybe they're familiar with the fact that SQLite has a way to do vector extensions.

342
00:37:41,510 --> 00:37:48,150
Can you pick at least one of those and explain how Quadrant is similar to and different from the system that you picked?

343
00:37:48,160 --> 00:37:50,150
Of course.

344
00:37:50,150 --> 00:37:55,589
I think PG vector is the best example to choose here just because that's the most common question that I'm getting.

345
00:37:55,589 --> 00:38:05,589
The main concern that people have when we discuss vector databases is that if you just add a new system into your existing stack and you already have a relational database such

346
00:38:05,589 --> 00:38:10,950
as posgress then you need to keep those two systems in sync somehow.

347
00:38:10,950 --> 00:38:16,870
So the main benefit of using PG vector in that case is that you don't really need to copy your data anywhere else.

348
00:38:16,870 --> 00:38:21,589
there is just a single system that keeps everything in one place.

349
00:38:21,589 --> 00:38:23,589
So that's quite often a concern of people that I'm speaking to.

350
00:38:23,589 --> 00:38:33,349
However, PG vector is just an extension of Postgress and Postgress is a relational database that like takes care about all these properties we just discussed.

351
00:38:33,349 --> 00:38:42,950
And since it's just an extension, it doesn't modify the the core of the system and it just acts as additional functionality of your relational database, which is okay.

352
00:38:42,960 --> 00:38:49,910
If you just deal with like thousands of of examples, then you shouldn't even notice any any difference.

353
00:38:49,910 --> 00:39:01,829
However, when we speak about higher scale systems like dealing with millions or even billions of vectors, the vector searched easily becomes a bottleneck of your relational database.

354
00:39:01,829 --> 00:39:07,750
Imagine you have a system that has like a million of documents in one of the tables.

355
00:39:07,750 --> 00:39:10,470
That's not that big amount of of information of data.

356
00:39:10,470 --> 00:39:19,750
If we speak about modern systems like there are so many transactional system that can handle this kind of load and it's not a big deal for posgress that for sure.

357
00:39:19,750 --> 00:39:28,150
However, if you decide to add this vector search capabilities and if you decide to use OpenAI embeddings for example,

358
00:39:28,150 --> 00:39:36,630
then this million vectors will transform to 6 GB of memory and vectors are typically stored in memory for the search to be just efficient.

359
00:39:36,630 --> 00:39:46,470
That means the vector search capabilities easily becomes the like the most important process inside your relational database even

360
00:39:46,470 --> 00:39:55,990
though it was supposed to handle like typical SQL queries like you will be selecting points based on their IDs or maybe some other typical filtering criteria

361
00:39:55,990 --> 00:40:05,030
but you are just generating an additional load on an existing system and from my experience that rarely works that well if you really reach a certain scale.

362
00:40:05,030 --> 00:40:09,670
Moreover, there are also some other issues that you may encounter just because PG vector is an extension.

363
00:40:09,670 --> 00:40:17,349
That means that if you want to search using vector search and at the same time perform filtering,

364
00:40:17,349 --> 00:40:23,910
coming back to the previous example, you want to filter items coming from a particular city, let's say New York,

365
00:40:23,910 --> 00:40:31,990
then it doesn't work that well on the semantics search level and that has to be expressed as traditional wear clause in SQL.

366
00:40:31,990 --> 00:40:37,270
However, in case of PG vector, you would be either using pre or post filtering,

367
00:40:37,280 --> 00:40:49,030
meaning that you either filter all the rows in your database that fulfills that criteria and then perform semantic search on top of them, which may end up as almost linear scan at some point.

368
00:40:49,030 --> 00:40:53,589
If you let's say 90% of your rows just match this criteria.

369
00:40:53,589 --> 00:41:02,550
And on the other hand, if you use post filtering, you are then running semantic search on all the rows you have and then you are filtering all this all this result.

370
00:41:02,550 --> 00:41:13,109
But that can also mean that you can end up with no results at all because the the set of points that you selected with semantic search just doesn't include any of the points from that particular city.

371
00:41:13,109 --> 00:41:26,069
And in case of quadrant we have a quite a unique approach to that because semantic search and metadata filtering are performed in a single pass because they are just incorporated into this helper data structures.

372
00:41:26,069 --> 00:41:28,230
So that's a huge difference.

373
00:41:28,230 --> 00:41:30,630
But also historically if we speak about search,

374
00:41:30,630 --> 00:41:38,230
anyone treating search seriously would probably set up a separate system for that such as elastic search

375
00:41:38,230 --> 00:41:47,990
or open search just because search requires different means than relational databases and they are built to support different use cases.

376
00:41:47,990 --> 00:41:50,230
The same applies to vector databases.

377
00:41:50,230 --> 00:41:54,870
I totally get the point of just using a single system when we are just experimenting

378
00:41:54,880 --> 00:42:09,430
and PG vector and SQite vector extensions are actually okay if you're just doing this kind of experiments but in a real production systems having a separate system for search makes a lot of sense for this reasons.

379
00:42:09,440 --> 00:42:11,510
Okay, thanks for that response.

380
00:42:11,510 --> 00:42:13,190
It was really thoughtprovoking.

381
00:42:13,190 --> 00:42:16,470
I wanted to pick up on three different words that you said.

382
00:42:16,470 --> 00:42:25,430
So first of all you mentioned the idea of hitting a bottleneck and then one of the bottlenecks that I heard you mention was related to the gigabytes of memory use.

383
00:42:25,430 --> 00:42:31,510
Then the other limitation or bottleneck was related to the fact that you would have to do a linear scan of some of the data.

384
00:42:31,510 --> 00:42:41,030
Just briefly are there other types of bottlenecks that a developer would bump into that would convince them hey I really need to use some type of vector similarity search engine?

385
00:42:41,040 --> 00:42:42,950
Yeah of course.

386
00:42:42,950 --> 00:42:46,710
So I'm glad you mentioned SQLite vector extension cuz actually this is something interesting.

387
00:42:46,720 --> 00:42:51,109
Like many people use SQLite for their site projects.

388
00:42:51,109 --> 00:42:58,390
Also some mature projects still use SQLite even though that's was supposed to be like an embedded database but rather for local usage.

389
00:42:58,390 --> 00:43:10,390
And this vector extension to SQLite is actually not an approximation of vector search but it's actually a brute force KN&amp;N that just compares your query embedding to all the document embeddings you have.

390
00:43:10,400 --> 00:43:21,829
Even if you have a look at their benchmarks, you can expect the latency to be as high as 9 seconds if you have just a million documents in your database.

391
00:43:21,829 --> 00:43:24,550
So that's okay if you just deal with hundreds or thousands rows.

392
00:43:24,560 --> 00:43:31,510
But like on high scale, you can expect it to be to be like the bottleneck of the whole system.

393
00:43:31,510 --> 00:43:38,309
And also using this naive approach, the brute force scan means that you don't really build any data structures for that.

394
00:43:38,309 --> 00:43:42,950
you just store the vectors on disk and then just sequentially load them from there.

395
00:43:42,950 --> 00:43:53,430
That also means that you can see that like the memory usage is not that high but the latency will be will be a complete disaster.

396
00:43:53,430 --> 00:43:59,430
So this kind of problems may occur when you really choose something which is not built on purpose to support vector search.

397
00:43:59,440 --> 00:44:01,109
Okay, that makes a lot of sense.

398
00:44:01,109 --> 00:44:07,430
What I'd like to do now is to transition our conversation to a new topic and I want to briefly discuss how someone would

399
00:44:07,440 --> 00:44:18,230
actually get started using Quadrant both from the perspective of running a Quadrant instance or accessing one of those instances and then also using one of the client libraries.

400
00:44:18,230 --> 00:44:28,470
So to get us started when I'm using Quadrant do I run it on my laptop or do I access a cloud version of it or do I deploy my own version in the cloud?

401
00:44:28,470 --> 00:44:32,470
Can you walk our listeners through some of the practical aspects of deploying Quadrant?

402
00:44:32,480 --> 00:44:34,150
Of course.

403
00:44:34,150 --> 00:44:36,390
So, there are different ways of how you can use Quadrant.

404
00:44:36,390 --> 00:44:38,550
We are an open-source engine.

405
00:44:38,550 --> 00:44:40,390
So, you can definitely run it on your laptop.

406
00:44:40,390 --> 00:44:45,270
And that's actually what I typically do when I just experiment with Quadrant.

407
00:44:45,270 --> 00:44:49,510
It's as easy as just pulling our Docker container running your on your machine.

408
00:44:49,520 --> 00:44:56,630
And functional wise, you are getting the same functionalities as you would get in the manage cloud.

409
00:44:56,630 --> 00:45:00,790
Like that's totally we are using the same containers in our cloud.

410
00:45:00,790 --> 00:45:05,510
The main benefit of using our manage cloud is that you get a really nice UI.

411
00:45:05,510 --> 00:45:07,990
You can spin up your clusters through the API that we have.

412
00:45:07,990 --> 00:45:14,550
So when you start to scale product, this is great cuz you don't really need to worry much about your infrastructure.

413
00:45:14,550 --> 00:45:22,150
You can totally focus on your product and let us focus on making your quadrant experience as seamless as possible.

414
00:45:22,150 --> 00:45:27,670
And there is also a third option except for on premise local usage or manage cloud.

415
00:45:27,670 --> 00:45:29,910
We also have a hybrid cloud offering.

416
00:45:29,910 --> 00:45:41,910
So hybrid cloud allows you to run your quadrant instances on your premises as long as you can provide us a Kubernetes cluster.

417
00:45:41,910 --> 00:45:55,589
So it's also great idea to use it that way if you already have all your systems running in on your own infrastructure that maybe even in cloud and you just want to bring quadrant as an additional component into an existing stack.

418
00:45:55,589 --> 00:46:00,390
We also provide the helm chart if you would like to run it on your kubernetes cluster I mean the open source version.

419
00:46:00,390 --> 00:46:11,270
So there are different ways of how to use it, but ultimately all of them will bring you the same quadrant experience cuz functionality is almost identical for all the possible modes of running it.

420
00:46:11,280 --> 00:46:13,349
Okay, thank you for that.

421
00:46:13,349 --> 00:46:18,950
So let's call the thing that we've just deployed the quadrant server. Is that an okay phrase to use for now?

422
00:46:18,960 --> 00:46:21,270
That's what we use to describe.

423
00:46:21,270 --> 00:46:28,150
So now that I have the Quadrant server running, which could be in a Docker container on my laptop or hybrid or cloud,

424
00:46:28,150 --> 00:46:35,510
I guess I need to run some type of Quadrant client, which is going to allow me to like extract the data from my documents,

425
00:46:35,520 --> 00:46:45,910
maybe using chunking like you mentioned a moment ago, and then I actually have to put it into the quadrant vector similarity search engine that's running in my server.

426
00:46:45,910 --> 00:46:51,030
So I know that there are Python Go and Rust libraries that are helping people to build the clients.

427
00:46:51,030 --> 00:46:57,349
Can you talk a little bit more about how developers would use these client libraries to interact with the Quadrant server?

428
00:46:57,359 --> 00:46:59,270
Of course.

429
00:46:59,270 --> 00:47:09,349
So all of these clients are actually some interfaces built on top of HTTP and gRPC protocols because that's what Quadrant exposes in the first place.

430
00:47:09,349 --> 00:47:23,190
However, the most popular client is our Python SDK and it comes with some additional benefits because you can interact with both protocols using the same interfaces and that's fine if you let's say have some restrictions and let's say at this point

431
00:47:23,190 --> 00:47:37,510
you can't use the gRPC protocol because it's just not allowed in the network you are operating on then you can still use quadrant in the HTTP mode and eventually switch to gRPC because it's just a bit more efficient once this is all solved.

432
00:47:37,510 --> 00:47:43,430
So those libraries are actually some thin wrappers around our HTTP and gRPC APIs.

433
00:47:43,440 --> 00:47:48,069
That just makes things a bit easier cuz we take care of the batching.

434
00:47:48,069 --> 00:47:56,630
When you insert your data with our clients, you can expect them to just send them in batches and that's a good practice to do that.

435
00:47:56,630 --> 00:48:05,910
Retries might be handled automatically, but overall you will be calling the methods which are named similarly to the HTTP endpoints for example.

436
00:48:05,910 --> 00:48:14,790
So that's what you typically do and that depends on on the language that you choose because some of them may have synchronous and asynchronous versions of the methods.

437
00:48:14,790 --> 00:48:22,150
So so that really depends on the platform but eventually you can also use HTTP or gRPC protocols directly depending on the platform you are working with.

438
00:48:22,160 --> 00:48:23,829
Okay.

439
00:48:23,829 --> 00:48:31,270
So what you're saying is that whether I'm using Python Go or Rust I have two protocol choices in terms of how I interact with the Quadrant server.

440
00:48:31,280 --> 00:48:36,150
Just very quickly, when I was using the Python client SDK myself,

441
00:48:36,150 --> 00:48:46,390
essentially what I did was I created a virtual environment and then used UV in order to install the Quadrant client as a dependency.

442
00:48:46,390 --> 00:48:50,150
What I also did next was actually use something like sentence transformers to create my embeddings.

443
00:48:50,160 --> 00:49:02,391
But just to make sure I'm clear, Quadrant doesn't technically care whether I use sentence transformers or open AI or any other way to create my embeddings. Did I get that correct?

444
00:49:02,400 --> 00:49:04,230
Yes, that's totally right.

445
00:49:04,230 --> 00:49:08,230
Actually, we do not assume that you will be using a particular model to encode your data.

446
00:49:08,240 --> 00:49:14,230
Many of our users at some point decide to fine-tune their own models.

447
00:49:14,230 --> 00:49:18,549
So, so they reflect their domain a a little bit better.

448
00:49:18,549 --> 00:49:22,950
So like we can't be really supporting a very particular set of models, quadrant is model agnostic.

449
00:49:22,950 --> 00:49:31,109
So so no matter how you create your vectors as long as you can provide them as a list of floats, it's fine to to use them with quadrant.

450
00:49:31,119 --> 00:49:32,791
Okay, thanks. That was awesome.

451
00:49:32,791 --> 00:49:34,790
So I can pick Python Go or Rust.

452
00:49:34,790 --> 00:49:38,630
I can pick from a very wide variety of embedding libraries.

453
00:49:38,630 --> 00:49:50,630
And then based on what you said just a moment ago, I can even do some fine-tuning of the embedding library for the specific type of data that I care about like markdown files or source code or PDFs or other things of that nature.

454
00:49:50,640 --> 00:50:00,309
Yes, many of our users just start with some existing models and then at some point they decide to fine-tune something for their own purposes. So yes, you can do it.

455
00:50:00,319 --> 00:50:01,829
All right, that's awesome.

456
00:50:01,829 --> 00:50:05,670
What I want to do now, since you mentioned the idea of fine-tuning,

457
00:50:05,670 --> 00:50:17,589
I'd like to talk a little bit about some of your experiences that you and other members of the Quadrant team have had when it comes to things like building or testing or doing performance evaluation for Quadrant.

458
00:50:17,589 --> 00:50:29,829
So, I wanted to start by asking you to share a story maybe of like a challenging bug or performance issue that you faced when you were developing Quadrant and then could you tell our listeners a little bit more about how you and the team solved that issue?

459
00:50:29,839 --> 00:50:31,589
Of course.

460
00:50:31,589 --> 00:50:33,430
This is actually described in our website.

461
00:50:33,430 --> 00:50:35,990
We have a pretty nice article describing that.

462
00:50:35,990 --> 00:50:38,790
For those of you who have some Rust experience,

463
00:50:38,790 --> 00:50:51,511
you probably have heard about Rox DB which is embedded key value store and it has been a key cornerstone for us to persist mutable data on disk for a pretty long time. It has one major problem though.

464
00:50:51,520 --> 00:50:54,470
It requires periodic compaction of data.

465
00:50:54,480 --> 00:51:03,109
That also means we need to do some sort of housekeeping to structure the data and to drop some old data for example.

466
00:51:03,119 --> 00:51:14,230
So whenever this compaction job runs it can block everything else and cause some latency spikes similarly to the garbage collector and we had no control over it.

467
00:51:14,240 --> 00:51:23,109
So that's also why we decided to implement something different a custom key value store which we called greet store.

468
00:51:23,109 --> 00:51:24,790
It's also like an open-source site project of Quadrron.

469
00:51:24,790 --> 00:51:27,670
You can find it on our GitHub repositories.

470
00:51:27,670 --> 00:51:35,349
And although like Rox DB is a fantastic general purpose product, there latency spikes were unacceptable.

471
00:51:35,349 --> 00:51:37,750
So we had to do that.

472
00:51:37,750 --> 00:51:45,430
And it has similar functionality to to Rox DB, but it's just a specialized for our specific use case.

473
00:51:45,430 --> 00:51:49,670
So that actually improved the the latency perceived by the users significantly.

474
00:51:49,670 --> 00:51:54,710
You can also find some benchmarks website that that proves that.

475
00:51:54,710 --> 00:52:00,630
So it like definitely that's something that we are really proud of and we also kept the back compatibility.

476
00:52:00,640 --> 00:52:08,950
So even though you were using the version that was still using Rox DB, you can upgrade to the latest one and still expect it to work.

477
00:52:08,950 --> 00:52:13,270
So that was definitely a challenge that we successfully solved in the recent months.

478
00:52:13,280 --> 00:52:16,550
Thanks for sharing that thoughtprovoking example. That's fascinating.

479
00:52:16,550 --> 00:52:25,430
We'll make sure to link in the show notes the blog post that you mentioned a moment ago so others can learn about this challenging story and this successful outcome.

480
00:52:25,430 --> 00:52:27,430
As we draw our episode to a conclusion,

481
00:52:27,430 --> 00:52:41,030
I'm wondering if you could comment briefly on the ways in which building and testing and evaluating the performance of a vector similarity search engine is different from other types of software systems with which you have experience.

482
00:52:41,040 --> 00:52:42,549
Yeah, definitely.

483
00:52:42,549 --> 00:52:47,589
I think that might be also interesting for those who would like to join quadrant core team.

484
00:52:47,589 --> 00:52:54,549
So I know that the core team works like they always try to keep the development momentum.

485
00:52:54,549 --> 00:53:02,470
So they implement features in small steps from inside out so they can merge them into the main branch quickly without having many diverging versions.

486
00:53:02,480 --> 00:53:06,950
It also makes the reviews and collaboration easier.

487
00:53:06,950 --> 00:53:12,549
So in case of vector databases, I think like testing is key, but we try not to overdo it.

488
00:53:12,559 --> 00:53:22,710
It's not only to prove that a particular feature works now, but we also try to prove that it won't break in the future when we decide to change something.

489
00:53:22,710 --> 00:53:37,030
We also try to handle all the common cases in end to end tests and also try to keep the test code minimal so it's not like 10 times bigger than the code for the feature itself.

490
00:53:37,030 --> 00:53:47,510
And in our case benchmarking is really hard because you can't really benchmark like an individual feature on some artificial data sets but you really need to think about real world use cases.

491
00:53:47,510 --> 00:53:51,990
The good thing is that we have lots of users already.

492
00:53:51,990 --> 00:53:57,349
So we can also build our test cases based on that and our benchmarks too.

493
00:53:57,349 --> 00:54:05,670
So we also like started to recommend doing some custom benchmarks once the requirements are clear cuz there are so many diverse way of how people can use vector search

494
00:54:05,670 --> 00:54:14,150
and that's something that we that we've learned and building distributed systems is really hard.

495
00:54:14,150 --> 00:54:21,510
That's where we struggled a lot and yeah I think we are just getting better in building them even though our our core team is really not that big.

496
00:54:21,520 --> 00:54:23,349
Thanks for that response.

497
00:54:23,349 --> 00:54:26,790
Now we've talked a lot in this episode about different key concepts.

498
00:54:26,790 --> 00:54:35,349
So we talked about vector embeddings and similarity search and we've gone through many of the details about both how you use Quadrant

499
00:54:35,349 --> 00:54:42,950
but then also about how you actually have gone to build quadrant or to do the benchmarking associated with quadrant. at the very end of our discussion.

500
00:54:42,950 --> 00:54:52,790
Now, I'm wondering if you could comment briefly on what you see as like the future of vector databases and their overall role in what we might call the AI or machine learning landscape.

501
00:54:52,800 --> 00:54:56,950
Well, definitely vector databases are not dead.

502
00:54:56,950 --> 00:55:04,470
Even though I've seen like multiple posts on LinkedIn about like the end of of the whole industry, the main problem of of AI or LLMs,

503
00:55:04,470 --> 00:55:09,829
I know that we use these two terms are terms as synonyms,

504
00:55:09,829 --> 00:55:20,790
but even the latest LLM can suffer from knowledge cutoff because they were trained and on some specific data sets and definitely don't know the most recent news and none of them could have been trained on your own data.

505
00:55:20,790 --> 00:55:29,990
So definitely some sort of retrieval is needed and vector databases will definitely serve that functionality for them

506
00:55:29,990 --> 00:55:43,670
because semantic search is just so well suited for natural language like search or multimodel rack because that's also something that started to be implemented recently and vector search is not only important in terms of rack

507
00:55:43,680 --> 00:55:55,030
or llms but nearest neighbor search is just such a versatile method that can solve multiple problems that I still feel

508
00:55:55,030 --> 00:55:58,710
that it's too early to say like what are what would be the typical use cases in the upcoming two or three years

509
00:55:58,720 --> 00:56:10,470
but I feel like many of us will start implementing something more than just which we've alumented generation and we will definitely see the applications of vector search

510
00:56:10,470 --> 00:56:25,270
for example as some sort of guard rails before we input any data into NLM because we can perform anomaly detection with nearest neighbor search easily and I'm looking forward to seeing some new use cases for that and pretty sure that's definitely going to happen.

511
00:56:25,280 --> 00:56:35,430
Okay, that makes sense and I can say from my own experience associated with using Quadrant, it is flexible and it can handle a wide variety of different documents.

512
00:56:35,430 --> 00:56:41,510
So I do think it's an area where there's still a lot of growth and the point that you made previously was a good one in regards

513
00:56:41,510 --> 00:56:52,390
to the fact that you often want your own standalone system for similarity search so that you can let the database that's relational do what it's good at and then have another system that can do what it's good at.

514
00:56:52,390 --> 00:57:01,589
So with all of those points in mind and the thoughtful insights that you've shared so far, are there any additional topics that we didn't cover that you think we should briefly discuss?

515
00:57:01,589 --> 00:57:05,349
I think we should mention the importance of evaluation.

516
00:57:05,349 --> 00:57:12,870
That's something that people tend to ignore when they build retrieval augmented generation or vector search in general.

517
00:57:12,870 --> 00:57:15,670
However, retrieval or search is not a new topic.

518
00:57:15,670 --> 00:57:20,950
We've been discussing proper ways to to do retrieval and evaluate it for ages.

519
00:57:20,950 --> 00:57:27,910
And even though you may choose the best performing embedding model from the public leaderboards or choose the best LLM,

520
00:57:27,920 --> 00:57:36,630
your system may just struggle with your specific data because none of these models was trained on something that would resemble it.

521
00:57:36,630 --> 00:57:42,309
So unless you are experimenting and I don't know doing like a side project over a weekend,

522
00:57:42,309 --> 00:57:53,910
it's always a good idea to start your semantic search journey with building well curated ground data set that will serve as a quality judge.

523
00:57:53,910 --> 00:57:56,630
So you can see whether your retrieval is really doing a great job.

524
00:57:56,640 --> 00:57:59,991
Thanks for that comment about evaluation. That makes a lot of sense.

525
00:58:00,000 --> 00:58:10,950
As we draw our episode to a conclusion, I'm wondering if you have a call to action for the listeners of Software Engineering Radio who want to learn more about Quadrant or get up and running and actually start to use it.

526
00:58:10,960 --> 00:58:18,549
Definitely, I invite you to our regular webinars that we organize every single month.

527
00:58:18,549 --> 00:58:29,910
And please just check out the quadrant cloud offering especially the cloud inference which actually makes things a bit easier cuz you really need to support and host your own embedding model.

528
00:58:29,910 --> 00:58:40,390
But you can send your data directly either text or images and expect the server to create the vectors without you worrying about hosting a model especially if you have no experience in that.

529
00:58:40,400 --> 00:58:42,549
Thanks Casper.

530
00:58:42,549 --> 00:58:46,710
Hey, it's really been fun to have this conversation on software engineering radio.

531
00:58:46,710 --> 00:58:50,870
I really appreciate you being here and devoting all this time to tell us about the quadrant database.

532
00:58:50,880 --> 00:58:54,230
Thank you, Greg. That was a great pleasure to be here with you today.

